  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 253 PM MDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nSynoptically for the long term portion of the forecast, the \nridge will begin to propagate eastward and will begin to stall \nout over the Rocky Mountain and Great Plains region. Looking at \nthe mid levels guidance does have some agreement on there being \nsome shortwave moving through the area begin Saturday through \nTuesday. \n\nOverall for this period, it looks to remain fairly dry and hot.\nThe daily temperatures for the weekend and early next week are \nforecast to be in the high 90s and low 100s. The days with the \nhighest temperatures look to be Saturday through Monday. \n\nStarting with Saturday, Guidance is suggesting that Dundy, \nHitchcock, and Red Willow Counties will have around a 30-50% of \nexceeding 100 degrees. The NBM and LREF however do show some \ndisagreement with the rest of the County Warning Area (CWA). The\nNBM is favoring the highest temperatures to stay in the \nNebraska counties whereas, the LREF favors areas east of CO Hwy \n385 having a 30-50% of seeing above 100 for highs. As for \nSunday, the LREF has the majority of the CWA having around a \n25-75% of exceeding 100 degrees with the higher values in the \neastern portions of the CWA. The NBM has similar coverage with \nareas east of CO Hwy 385 but values in the 20-60%. Monday \nGuidance is in agreement more than previous days and is showing \nthat there is around a 20-45% chance of seeing temperatures \nhigher than 100 degrees. The main areas for seeing these \ntemperatures look to be east of KS Hwy 25/83 and south of US Hwy\n40.\n\nMoving to precipation chances, the highest days to see \nprecipitation chances is Monday and Tuesday. Currently for each \nday PoPs are in the range of 20-30% for areas west of KS Hwy 83.\nLooking at SFC-CAPE guidance is suggesting there to be around \n1000-1500 J/kg. SFC-500 mb Shear there is around 20-30 kts which\nwould suggest storms to be unorganized if they are able to \ndevelop. Given this is around a week out and will a fair amount \ncan change.",
